        <!-- SECTION 1 -->
        <div id="what-it-does" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. What Basement Waterproofing Paint Actually Does
          </h2>

          <p>
            Concrete and concrete block are porous. Water in the soil outside your foundation migrates through those pores by capillary action, arrives at the inside face, and evaporates into the room — carrying dissolved salts with it, which is what leaves the white chalky bloom known as efflorescence. That process is why a basement smells musty, why the walls feel cold and slightly damp, and why cardboard boxes stored against them go soft. It happens with no visible leak anywhere.
          </p>

          <p>
            A masonry waterproofer stops that specific mechanism. It penetrates into the open pores of the wall and blocks them, so capillary migration cannot continue. The better products go further: a cementitious formulation actually reacts with the alkaline compounds in the concrete and becomes chemically part of the wall, which is what lets manufacturers publish a hydrostatic pressure rating — a figure for how much water pressure the coating can resist before it is pushed off the substrate.
          </p>

          <p>
            What it does not do — and cannot do, regardless of price — is hold back water that is arriving in volume. If the perimeter drain is blocked, if the downspouts discharge next to the foundation, or if the water table rises above the slab after heavy rain, water is under real pressure, and pressure finds the weakest point. A coating on the inside face is the last thing in the path and the least able to resist. Read our guide to <a href="/wet-basement-solutions/">wet basement solutions</a> if that describes your situation; nothing on this page is the fix.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
