        <div id="types" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. The Four Types of Basement Waterproofing Paint
          </h2>

          <p>
            The products sold under this heading use four quite different mechanisms, and matching the mechanism to your wall matters far more than choosing between brands within a type.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Cementitious Latex Waterproofers</h3>
          <p>
            Portland cement suspended in a latex binder. Brushed into bare masonry, the cement fraction reacts with the wall and the coating becomes structurally integrated with it. This is the category with real hydrostatic pressure ratings and long warranties, and it is where DRYLOK sits. The finish is slightly textured and gritty, the prep requirements are the strictest of any type, and the performance in return is the best available from the inside of a wall.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Acrylic Masonry Waterproofers</h3>
          <p>
            Acrylic resin films that block moisture and resist alkali attack from fresh masonry. They apply far more easily, cover better, tint to a wider range of colours, and leave a smooth, paint-like finish suitable for a finished room. They resist dampness competently and pressure only modestly. KILZ and Behr's basement products are the mainstream examples.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Elastomeric Liquid Rubber</h3>
          <p>
            High-solids rubber membranes that cure flexible and stretch rather than crack when the wall moves. They are the only sensible category for a wall with a history of hairline cracking, they tolerate damp substrates, and they can be used below grade on the exterior. They cost more, build thicker, and leave a rubbery surface that will not take ordinary wall paint. Our <a href="/liquid-rubber-vs-drylok/">liquid rubber vs DRYLOK comparison</a> covers the choice in detail.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Penetrating Reactive Sealers</h3>
          <p>
            Sodium silicate solutions that soak in and crystallise inside the concrete's capillary structure, sealing it from within with nothing left on the surface. Permanent, invisible, paintable afterwards — and effective only on dense poured concrete or tight block, because coarse porous block does not give the reaction anything to work with.
          </p>

