        <div id="how-to-apply" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. How to Apply Basement Waterproofing Paint Properly
          </h2>

          <p>
            Preparation is not a preamble to this job — it is the job. Every failure we have seen or been asked about traces back to one of the first three steps below.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Step 1: Get Back to Bare Masonry</h3>
          <p>
            Any existing paint has to come off. Cementitious waterproofers bond by reacting with the masonry itself, and a film of old latex between the two prevents that entirely — the coating goes on beautifully and lifts in sheets a few months later. Use a paint stripper suitable for masonry or a grinder with a dust shroud, and accept that this is the slowest part of the project. If a previous owner painted the walls, budget a full day for a modest basement.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Step 2: Kill the Efflorescence</h3>
          <p>
            That white powder is mineral salt deposited by evaporating water, and it is not a surface the coating can bond to. Wire-brush it off dry, then vacuum. Do not wash it off with water first — the water dissolves the salts and drives them back into the wall, where they reappear later, often through your new coating. If the wall is heavily contaminated, an efflorescence remover followed by a thorough rinse and a proper drying period is worth the extra day.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Step 3: Fill Cracks and Holes First</h3>
          <p>
            Waterproofing paint seals pores, not gaps. Any crack wider than a hairline needs hydraulic cement, chased out into a V-groove first so the repair has something to grip. An actively leaking crack needs a polyurethane injection kit rather than a surface patch. Let the repair cure fully before coating. Our <a href="/basement-crack-repair/">basement crack repair guide</a> covers the technique.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Step 4: Brush the First Coat, Do Not Roll It</h3>
          <p>
            The first coat's purpose is to fill the pores, and a roller bridges over them rather than filling them. Use a stiff masonry brush and work the material in with pressure, in overlapping horizontal strokes. It is slower and harder than rolling and it is the difference between a coating that performs and one that leaks through pinholes. Keep a wet edge across each section.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Step 5: Second Coat After a Full Recoat Window</h3>
          <p>
            Wait the full time on the label, usually twenty-four hours. Apply the second coat in vertical strokes, crossing the first, which makes missed spots obvious. This coat can be rolled. Every hydrostatic pressure rating published for these products assumes two coats; one coat is a damp-proofer at best.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-amber-50 border border-amber-200 rounded-2xl p-6 my-8">
            <p class="text-sm font-black text-amber-800 mb-2">Pro Tip: Coat the Wall When It Is Coolest</p>
            <p class="text-sm text-amber-700">
              A basement wall in late spring can be warm enough on the surface to flash the coating off before it penetrates, which leaves a weak, chalky layer. Early morning, or a cool spell, gives the material more time to soak into the pores. Ventilate the space while you work and for the full cure period afterwards — even the low-odour water-based products need airflow to cure properly in an enclosed room.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- SECTION 5 -->
        <div id="when-paint-is-wrong" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. When Waterproofing Paint Is the Wrong Answer
          </h2>

          <p>
            Being straight about the limits is the most useful thing this page can do. There are three situations where buying a coating is buying the wrong thing.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Water Arrives After Rain</h3>
          <p>
            If the basement is dry most of the time and wet within hours of heavy rain, the problem is surface water reaching the foundation, and it is solved outside: clean gutters, downspouts discharging several feet from the house, and soil graded away from the wall rather than towards it. These fixes cost very little and solve a startling proportion of wet basements. A coating applied before them is money spent on the symptom.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Water Comes Up Through the Floor</h3>
          <p>
            Water appearing at the wall-floor joint or through the slab is a rising water table, which no wall coating addresses. The answer is an interior perimeter drain and a sump, or exterior drainage. See <a href="/water-coming-through-basement-floor/">water coming through the basement floor</a> for the diagnostic steps.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">The Wall Is Structurally Cracked</h3>
          <p>
            Horizontal cracking in a block wall, stair-stepping through mortar joints, or a wall that is visibly bowing is a structural matter, not a moisture one. Coating over it hides the evidence of an ongoing problem. Get it looked at before anything goes on the surface.
          </p>

          <p>
            The corollary is worth stating plainly, because the internet's cynicism about these products has overshot: if your walls are simply damp, musty, and blooming efflorescence, with no standing water and no rain-driven flooding, waterproofing paint is genuinely effective and among the cheapest meaningful improvements available. That describes a large share of older basements.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- SECTION 6 -->
        <div id="cost" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. What the Job Actually Costs
          </h2>

          <p>
            Coverage on masonry is much lower than on drywall, and this is where budgets go wrong. A gallon of cementitious waterproofer covers roughly 75 to 100 square feet per coat on block — call it 40 to 50 square feet of finished wall once you account for two coats. A basement with 120 linear feet of wall at seven feet high is around 840 square feet, which means somewhere in the region of 16 to 20 gallons for two coats.
          </p>

          <p>
            At typical prices that is a materials bill in the several-hundred-dollar range, before the stripper, brushes, hydraulic cement and the shop vac filters you will get through. It is still a fraction of what a contracted interior waterproofing system costs, and our <a href="/basement-waterproofing-cost/">basement waterproofing cost guide</a> puts the numbers side by side. The labour is the real expense if you are not doing it yourself: the preparation, not the painting, is what fills the hours.
          </p>

          <p>
            One economy worth taking: you do not have to do the entire basement at once. Damp problems are rarely uniform, and treating the two worst walls properly beats treating four walls thinly. One wall, prepped correctly and given two full coats, will outperform a whole basement given one rushed coat over unstripped paint every time.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="common-mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            Common Mistakes With Basement Waterproofing Paint
          </h2>

          <p>
            Five failures account for nearly every disappointed review these products receive.
          </p>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Applying Over Existing Paint</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The single biggest cause of failure. Cementitious waterproofers must bond to bare masonry. Over old paint they peel in sheets, usually within a season, and the product gets the blame for a preparation shortcut.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Stopping at One Coat</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Every published pressure rating assumes two full coats. The first fills the pores; the second builds the continuous barrier. One coat looks finished and performs like a damp-proofer.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Washing Efflorescence Off With Water</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Water dissolves the salts and carries them back into the wall, where they crystallise again later — sometimes pushing your new coating off from behind. Brush it off dry and vacuum, or use a proper efflorescence remover and allow a real drying period.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Coating Over an Unfilled Crack</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                These products seal pores, not gaps. Paint spanning an open crack cracks with it. Fill first with hydraulic cement, or inject an active leak with polyurethane, and let the repair cure before coating.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Treating a Drainage Problem With a Brush</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Water under pressure from outside will find the weakest point, and an interior coating is the last and weakest thing in its path. Fix gutters, downspouts and grading first. If water still arrives, the answer is drainage, not a better coating.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best basement waterproofing paint?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                DRYLOK Extreme for most walls: 15 psi hydrostatic rating, a ten-year warranty on properly prepped masonry, and available everywhere. For a wall that cracks or moves seasonally, an elastomeric such as Ames Blue Max is the better mechanism. For a basement being finished as living space with no seepage history, KILZ Basement &amp; Masonry gives a smoother, more paintable result.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does basement waterproofing paint actually work?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For the problem it targets, yes. Moisture migrating through porous masonry — damp walls, musty smell, efflorescence — is exactly what these coatings stop, and on properly prepared bare masonry in two coats they stop it reliably for years. What they cannot do is resist water arriving under pressure from failed drainage or a high water table.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How many coats of waterproofing paint do you need?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Two, without exception. The published pressure ratings are based on two full coats. Brush the first in hard to fill the pores, wait the full recoat window — usually 24 hours — then apply the second, crossing the direction of the first so misses show up. A single coat leaves pinholes that water finds.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can you paint over basement waterproofing paint?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes for cementitious and acrylic waterproofers once fully cured, using a masonry-suitable topcoat — although most are tintable, so it is often unnecessary. Elastomeric liquid rubber is the exception: the cured rubbery surface will not hold ordinary wall paint, so finished rooms need framing and board over it instead.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can you apply waterproofing paint over existing paint?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                No — and this is the most common reason these products fail. Cementitious waterproofers bond by reacting with open masonry pores, and any paint film blocks that reaction. Strip or grind the old coating off, wire-brush efflorescence away, vacuum thoroughly, then coat. Acrylic waterproofers are marginally more tolerant but perform far better on bare masonry too.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long does basement waterproofing paint last?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                On bare, properly prepared masonry, a cementitious waterproofer commonly lasts well beyond a decade, and DRYLOK Extreme is warranted for ten years. Acrylics have a shorter life under real moisture load. Preparation dominates the outcome — a coating applied over residue fails within a season no matter what it cost.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is waterproofing paint enough, or do I need drainage?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                If water runs in, pools on the floor, or appears after every heavy rain, no coating fixes it. Start outside: gutters, downspout extensions and grading away from the foundation. If water persists, the answer is a perimeter drain and sump. Paint is the right answer only for damp, musty walls and slow seepage through the masonry itself.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do you need to seal cracks before waterproofing paint?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes. These coatings seal pores rather than bridging gaps, so paint over an unfilled crack simply cracks. Chase the crack into a V-groove and fill with hydraulic cement, or use a polyurethane injection kit if it is actively leaking, and let the repair cure fully before the first coat goes on.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

