          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            For a damp, musty basement with no standing water, <strong class="text-white">DRYLOK Extreme</strong> remains the coating to beat — the pressure rating, the warranty and the availability are all real. Choose <strong class="text-white">Ames Blue Max</strong> instead if the wall cracks, <strong class="text-white">KILZ Basement &amp; Masonry</strong> if the room is being finished and the wall has never been wet, and <strong class="text-white">RadonSeal</strong> if the wall is dense poured concrete and you would rather seal it from within than coat it.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed">
            Then spend your effort where it actually decides the outcome: strip back to bare masonry, brush efflorescence off dry, fill every crack, and put on two full coats. A mid-priced product applied that way will outlast the most expensive coating on the shelf rolled thinly over old paint — and if water is arriving under pressure rather than seeping through pores, put the brush down and fix the drainage first.
